 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nColumn qualification fails when struct field access uses qualified table name\n\n#### Description\n\nWhen accessing struct fields with a qualified table name (e.g., `table.struct_field.nested_field`), the column qualification logic incorrectly handles the case where the root part is already a qualified table reference. This causes errors during SQL optimization.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport sqlglot\nfrom sqlglot import optimize\n\n# This should work but raises an error\nsql = \"\"\"\nSELECT t.my_struct.field1, t.my_struct.field2 \nFROM my_table t\n\"\"\"\n\ntry:\n    optimized = optimize(sql, dialect=\"spark\")\n    print(\"Success:\", optimized)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"Error:\", e)\n```\n\nThe issue occurs when the optimizer tries to qualify columns that are already partially qualified with table names, specifically when dealing with struct field access patterns like `table_alias.struct_column.field`.\n\n#### Expected Behavior\n\nThe column qualification should properly handle struct field access with qualified table names and not attempt to re-qualify already qualified references.\n\n#### Actual Behavior\n\nThe optimizer raises errors when encountering struct field access patterns that use qualified table names, preventing successful SQL optimization.\n</issue>\n",
